---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
import SvgFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/SvgFigure.astro"
import { series, stroke } from "~/data/palette"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="ladder-paradox"
  lead="The lengths change between observers. So does the timing of the doors."
>
  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    The ladder moves through the barn close to the speed of light. Both observers agree that the
    doors close, but not that they close at the same moment.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <Panel title="From the barn" accent="gold" class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-2">
      <SvgFigure
        name="ladder-barn-view"
        class="w-full max-w-md"
        viewBox="0 0 360 205"
        title="Barn's view: the short ladder fits and the closed-door periods overlap"
        desc="A 10 metre blue ladder fits completely inside a 12 metre gold barn. On two timelines, red bars show the near and far doors closed during the same brief period: they shut and reopen together in the barn's frame."
      >
        <g fill="currentColor" font-size="15">
          <text x="10" y="18" font-weight="700">
            Barn: 12 m
          </text>
          <rect
            x="10"
            y="30"
            width="340"
            height="34"
            fill={series.gold}
            fill-opacity="0.25"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1.5"
          />
          <rect x="38" y="40" width="284" height="14" fill={series.blue} />
          <line x1="10" x2="10" y1="27" y2="67" stroke={series.red} stroke-width="5" />
          <line x1="350" x2="350" y1="27" y2="67" stroke={series.red} stroke-width="5" />
          <text x="10" y="87">
            Ladder: 10 m — wholly inside
          </text>
          <text x="10" y="115" font-weight="700">
            Red = door closed
          </text>
          <text x="10" y="145">
            Near
          </text>
          <text x="10" y="175">
            Far
          </text>
          <path
            d="M88 140 H344 M88 170 H344"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-opacity="0.35"
            fill="none"
          />
          <rect x="214" y="132" width="46" height="16" fill={series.red} />
          <rect x="214" y="162" width="46" height="16" fill={series.red} />
          <line
            x1="214"
            x2="214"
            y1="130"
            y2="181"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-dasharray="3 3"
          />
          <line
            x1="260"
            x2="260"
            y1="130"
            y2="181"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-dasharray="3 3"
          />
          <text x="88" y="197" font-size="13">
            Earlier
          </text>
          <text x="344" y="197" text-anchor="end" font-size="13">
            Later →
          </text>
        </g>
      </SvgFigure>
      <p class="text-base leading-normal">
        Both doors close together, and reopen together. The red periods overlap while the
        shortened ladder is entirely inside.
      </p>
    </Panel>

    <Panel title="From the ladder" accent="blue" class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-2">
      <SvgFigure
        name="ladder-ladder-view"
        class="w-full max-w-md"
        viewBox="0 0 360 205"
        title="Ladder's view: the far door reopens before the near door closes"
        desc="The 20 metre ladder is longer than the 6 metre barn. Red bars show when each door is closed: the far-door bar finishes well before the near-door bar starts. There is never a moment when both doors are shut in the ladder's frame."
      >
        <g fill="currentColor" font-size="15">
          <text x="10" y="18" font-weight="700">
            Ladder: 20 m
          </text>
          <rect x="10" y="27" width="340" height="14" fill={series.blue} />
          <text x="10" y="65" font-weight="700">
            Barn: 6 m
          </text>
          <rect
            x="10"
            y="74"
            width="102"
            height="18"
            fill={series.gold}
            fill-opacity="0.25"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1.5"
          />
          <text x="10" y="115" font-weight="700">
            Red = door closed
          </text>
          <text x="10" y="145">
            Near
          </text>
          <text x="10" y="175">
            Far
          </text>
          <path
            d="M88 140 H344 M88 170 H344"
            stroke={stroke}
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-opacity="0.35"
            fill="none"
          />
          <rect x="274" y="132" width="50" height="16" fill={series.red} />
          <rect x="118" y="162" width="50" height="16" fill={series.red} />
          <text x="88" y="197" font-size="13">
            Earlier
          </text>
          <text x="344" y="197" text-anchor="end" font-size="13">
            Later →
          </text>
        </g>
      </SvgFigure>
      <p class="text-base leading-normal">
        The far door shuts first, then opens before the front of the ladder reaches it. The near
        door shuts later, after the back has passed. There is no moment when both doors are shut
        around the ladder.
      </p>
    </Panel>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The coloured bars compare lengths {" "}<strong>within</strong>{" "}each observer's frame; the red
    intervals below show when each door is closed. They overlap in the barn's frame, but not in
    the ladder's frame. These timing diagrams are schematic, not to scale. The paradox
    disappears because simultaneity is not shared.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
